  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "To take small steps 亍 with one's feet 彳"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a street intersection."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "人之步趨也。从彳从亍。凡行之屬皆从行。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形) – a street intersection. Original form of 行 (OC *ɡaːŋ, “place”). Later phonetically borrowed to represent 行 (OC *ɡaːŋs, “to go”).\nOriginally symmetric, it has been simplified asymmetrically; the left half 彳 (chì) is widely used as a radical, while the right half 亍 (chù) finds occasional use, and the character can be broken up as 彳 + 亍, though originally it was not a compound.\nSchuessler (2007) tentatively considers the word to be borrowed from Mon-Khmer; compare Khmer រង (rɔɔng, “ridge, gutter”). Note also similarities to Burmese ကျင့် (kyang., “to practice”) (Luce, 1981)."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "人之步趨也。从彳从亍。凡行之屬皆从行。",
  "shuowenExplanation": "step; hasten of man. Ideographic compound of 彳 \"step with left foot\" and 亍 \"to take small steps\" and 行 \"go\". All characters in the 行 \"go\" category use this as radical.",
